.TH tail "" "Command"
.SH NAME
tail \- print the end of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtail [+\-\fInumber\fB[bcfl]] [\fIfile\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
tail -- print the end of a file
Usage:	tail [ +number[bcl] ] [ file ]
	tail [ -number[bcl] ] [ file ]
Options:
	+	'number' is from beginning of file
	-	'number' is from end of file
	b	'number' is in blocks
	c	'number' is in characters (bytes)
	l	'number' is in lines (default)
.HE
.B tail
copies the last part of
.IR file ,
or of the standard input if none is named, onto the standard output.
If no
.I number
is given, the last ten lines are copied.
.PP
The optional first argument is a
.B +
or
.B \-
sign followed by a
.I number
and, optionally, one of the letters
.BR b ,
.BR c ,
or
.BR l .
A
.B \-
sign (the default) counts
.I number
backward from the end of the file; a
.B +
sign counts it forward from the beginning.
The letter
.B l
(the default) means
.I number
is a count of lines; a scale of
.B b
means 512-byte blocks and
.B c
means characters (bytes).
.PP
The letter
.B f
may follow the count, or the sign if there is no count; it selects
continuous read mode, described under
.B \-f
below.
.IP \fB+\fInumber\fR
Start copying
.I number
lines, blocks, or characters from the beginning of
.IR file .
.IP \fB\-\fInumber\fR
Start copying
.I number
lines, blocks, or characters back from the end of
.IR file .
.IP \fBb\fR
Interpret
.I number
as a count of 512-byte blocks.
.IP \fBc\fR
Interpret
.I number
as a count of characters (bytes).
.IP \fBl\fR
Interpret
.I number
as a count of lines.
This is the default if no scale letter is given.
.IP \fBf\fR
After copying up to the end of
.IR file ,
do not exit; instead keep reading the file and echo any data
subsequently appended to it, indefinitely.
This lets you watch a file grow.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR dd ,
.BR head ,
.B sed
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B tail
prints the message `Window too large' and exits if the requested count
is too large to hold in memory, and complains if
.I file
cannot be opened.
.SH NOTES
When seeking is not possible on the input (for example, a pipe),
.B tail
reads through the whole input a character at a time instead, which is
slow for a large trailing count.
